  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "If $sec\u2061x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then what is $\\sqrt{5}sec\u2061(x+20\\pi )-3?$",
  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2\\cdot 10\\cdot \\pi )=sec\u2061(x+20\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec\u2061x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then $sec\u2061(x+20\\pi )=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}sec\u2061(x+20\\pi )-3 & =\\sqrt{5}(2\\sqrt{5})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
